-- Laytime and demurrage - the shipping industry's oldest argument
-- about time, with the clock's rules enforced by the runtime instead
-- of by competing spreadsheets attached to angry emails.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain, in case chartering is not your world.
-- When a company hires a ship to carry a cargo, the deal (the
-- "charterparty") includes an allowance of time for loading and
-- unloading - the "laytime". Say five days. If the port work takes
-- longer than the allowance, the hirer pays the owner compensation
-- for every extra day, called "demurrage" - ships earn money by
-- moving, and a ship stuck at a berth is a ship not moving. Demurrage
-- is not a fine and not a breach; it is a priced, expected outcome,
-- settled to the minute.
--
-- And the minutes are exactly where the fighting starts. The clock
-- does not simply run from arrival to departure: it starts only after
-- the ship gives formal notice that she is ready (the "Notice of
-- Readiness", NOR), plus an agreed grace period ("turn time"); it
-- stops for the periods the charterparty excepts - rain that halts
-- the cranes, a port holiday; and both sides keep a minute-by-minute
-- log of events (the "Statement of Facts") that they later argue
-- over. A demurrage claim is an audit of that log. Whole departments
-- exist to fight them.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about it. The system stores *claims*
-- it has agreed to admit because they obey the rules - the tendering
-- of the NOR at an exact instant, the commencement of the clock, each
-- stretch of time that counted. Two kinds of rule do the work below:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. Here the invariants are the *bookkeeping truths*: an
--     interval's recorded length is exactly the gap between its ends;
--     no counted time exists before the clock started. Notice what is
--     deliberately NOT an invariant: using up the allowance. Running
--     over the laytime is the normal story - that is what demurrage
--     *is* - so the model must record it calmly, not forbid it.
--   * a `require` is a gate on one action, checked at the moment you
--     act. The clock may not start before the notice is tendered;
--     counted time may not be recorded before the clock starts. Gates
--     police the order of events; invariants keep the arithmetic
--     honest forever.
--
-- Money and cargo bring a third shape: a number that knows what it
-- counts. The cargo is so many tonnes; the demurrage rate is so many
-- dollars per day of delay. In this model those are not bare numbers
-- but quantities with a unit on them - `Decimal[t]`, `Decimal[USD]` -
-- and the runtime refuses to add, compare, or confuse amounts of
-- different units. Tonnes never meet dollars by accident; a sum of
-- settlements is a sum of dollars or it is rejected. The unit is a
-- label the rules respect, nothing more: the system knows no exchange
-- rates and no tonne-to-kilogram table, and "per day" is not a unit
-- here but a calculation you can read below - the daily amount times
-- the number of days, with the days computed exactly.
--
-- Time itself appears in two distinct shapes, and the distinction is
-- the lesson of this example. A `Timestamp` is an exact instant on
-- the shared UTC timeline - the moment the NOR was tendered. A
-- `Duration` is a length of time with no position - six hours of turn
-- time, three days of counted laytime. Instants subtract into
-- durations; durations add up and compare. (What this model does not
-- yet say is anything about *local* port time - the day that is 25
-- hours long when the clocks fall back. That interpretation enters as
-- admitted claims in this example's next stage, not as a hidden
-- runtime assumption.)

program laytime_demurrage

-- The vocabulary. Each `predicate` names a kind of claim this world
-- can hold: the fixture (the deal's agreed numbers), the notice, the
-- running clock, and each stretch of time that counted against the
-- allowance.
predicate VoyageFixture(voyage: Subject, vessel: Subject, port: Subject, allowed: Duration)
predicate NorTendered(nor: Subject, voyage: Subject, tendered_at: Timestamp)
predicate LaytimeCommenced(voyage: Subject, commenced_at: Timestamp)
predicate CountingInterval(interval: Subject, voyage: Subject, from: Timestamp, to: Timestamp, len: Duration)
predicate CargoOpsCompleted(voyage: Subject, completed_at: Timestamp)

-- The cargo book: what the vessel may carry, and each parcel booked
-- onto her, in tonnes. And the money book: the agreed daily demurrage
-- amount, and each settlement paid against the delay, in dollars.
predicate VesselCapacity(voyage: Subject, capacity: Decimal[t])
predicate CargoParcel(parcel: Subject, voyage: Subject, qty: Decimal[t])
predicate DemurrageRate(voyage: Subject, daily: Decimal[USD])
predicate DemurrageSettled(settlement: Subject, voyage: Subject, amount: Decimal[USD])

-- The read-side result both parties actually care about: how much
-- time, if any, ran past the allowance, shown beside the allowance it
-- ran past. Derived, never stored - see the bottom of the file.
predicate TimeOnDemurrage(voyage: Subject, allowed: Duration, excess: Duration)

-- What the delay is worth: the daily amount times the exact count of
-- days on demurrage. Also derived, never stored - and it shows the
-- figures it was computed from (the allowance, the daily amount)
-- beside the answer, the way a demurrage invoice does.
predicate DemurrageDue(voyage: Subject, allowed: Duration, daily: Decimal[USD], due: Decimal[USD])

-- Intents are messages to the outside world after a change commits -
-- the operations team learns the clock started; the demurrage desk
-- learns the cargo work is done and the counting can be argued about.
intent LaytimeClockStarted(voyage: Subject)
intent CargoOpsDone(voyage: Subject)
intent DemurrageSettlementRecorded(settlement: Subject)

-- The bookkeeping truths. If any change below would leave the world
-- violating one of these, the whole change is rejected.

-- An interval's recorded length is exactly the gap between its ends -
-- not "about", exactly. Anyone re-adding the Statement of Facts must
-- reach the same total, or the claim could never have been admitted.
invariant interval_length_is_its_span:
    CountingInterval(i, v, f, t, len) implies len = t - f

-- Time can only count after the clock has started, and never from
-- before it started. (The gate on `record_counting_interval` checks
-- the same thing at action time; this invariant makes it a permanent
-- property of the world that even a future, carelessly written
-- transformation cannot break.)
invariant counted_time_follows_commencement:
    CountingInterval(i, v, f, t, len) implies (LaytimeCommenced(v, c) and c at_or_before f)

-- The clock starts once per voyage. Two commencements would mean two
-- competing readings of every interval that follows.
invariant clock_starts_once:
    LaytimeCommenced(v, a) and LaytimeCommenced(v, b) implies a = b

-- One notice per voyage in this simplified model. (Real charterparties
-- argue about re-tendered notices; that argument arrives with the
-- Statement-of-Facts corrections in this example's next stage.)
invariant one_notice_per_voyage:
    NorTendered(a, v, _) and NorTendered(b, v, _) implies a = b

-- The cargo never exceeds what the vessel can carry. Tonnes against
-- tonnes: the comparison is only possible because both sides carry
-- the same unit, and the sum opens at the zero-tonne seed admitted
-- with the capacity (a sum over nothing is a bare number, and a bare
-- number does not compare with tonnes - so the book is never empty).
invariant cargo_within_capacity:
    VesselCapacity(v, cap) implies sum(qty | CargoParcel(_, v, qty)) <= cap

-- Settlements never exceed what the delay is worth. The right-hand
-- side recomputes the due figure from first principles - the counted
-- time past the allowance, in exact days, times the daily amount - so
-- there is no stored "amount due" to drift or to quietly edit. The
-- rule only takes hold once the clock has started: before that there
-- is no counted time to price.
invariant settlements_within_due:
    ((DemurrageRate(v, daily) and VoyageFixture(v, _, _, allowed) and LaytimeCommenced(v, _)) implies
        sum(amount | DemurrageSettled(_, v, amount)) <=
            daily * (max(sum(len | CountingInterval(_, v, _, _, len)) - allowed, duration(PT0S)) / duration(PT24H)))

-- The only ways state may change.

-- Fix the voyage: the deal's numbers, agreed before the ship sails.
-- The allowance arrives as a duration - `PT120H` is five days - and
-- stays untouched by everything that follows; the question is only
-- ever how the counted time compares to it.
transformation fix_voyage(voyage, vessel, port, allowed):
    require not VoyageFixture(voyage, _, _, _)
    admit VoyageFixture(voyage, vessel, port, allowed)

-- The master tenders Notice of Readiness: a formal "we are here and
-- ready", at an exact instant. Everything downstream hangs off this
-- moment, which is why it is a first-class claim with its own id.
transformation tender_nor(nor, voyage, tendered_at):
    require VoyageFixture(voyage, _, _, _)
    require not NorTendered(_, voyage, _)
    admit NorTendered(nor, voyage, tendered_at)

-- Start the clock. The charterparty grants six hours of turn time
-- after the notice, so commencement is *computed*: the tendering
-- instant shifted by an exact duration. Note the zero-length seed
-- interval admitted alongside: it opens the count at zero, so the
-- running total below is never a sum over nothing.
transformation commence_laytime(voyage, seed):
    bind NorTendered(_, voyage, tendered_at)
    require not LaytimeCommenced(voyage, _)
    let commenced_at = tendered_at + duration(PT6H)
    admit LaytimeCommenced(voyage, commenced_at)
    admit CountingInterval(seed, voyage, commenced_at, commenced_at, duration(PT0S))
    emit LaytimeClockStarted(voyage)

-- Record a stretch of time that counted against the allowance - the
-- Statement of Facts, one line at a time. The gates police the order
-- of events: the clock must be running, the stretch must start no
-- earlier than commencement, and it must not end before it begins.
-- The length is computed from the ends, and the invariant above
-- guarantees it can never drift from them.
transformation record_counting_interval(interval, voyage, from, to):
    bind LaytimeCommenced(voyage, commenced_at)
    require commenced_at at_or_before from
    require from at_or_before to
    let len = to - from
    admit CountingInterval(interval, voyage, from, to, len)

-- Cargo work is done. From this instant the counted total is final
-- (in this stage of the model) and the demurrage conversation begins.
transformation complete_cargo_ops(voyage, completed_at):
    bind LaytimeCommenced(voyage, commenced_at)
    require commenced_at at_or_before completed_at
    require not CargoOpsCompleted(voyage, _)
    admit CargoOpsCompleted(voyage, completed_at)
    emit CargoOpsDone(voyage)

-- Declare what the vessel can carry. The zero-tonne seed parcel
-- admitted alongside opens the cargo book, exactly as the zero-length
-- interval opens the time book at commencement.
transformation declare_capacity(voyage, seed_parcel, capacity):
    require VoyageFixture(voyage, _, _, _)
    require not VesselCapacity(voyage, _)
    admit VesselCapacity(voyage, capacity)
    admit CargoParcel(seed_parcel, voyage, 0 t)

-- Book a parcel of cargo. The gate asks only that the capacity has
-- been declared; the invariant above decides whether this parcel
-- still fits, against everything already booked.
transformation load_parcel(parcel, voyage, qty):
    require VesselCapacity(voyage, _)
    admit CargoParcel(parcel, voyage, qty)

-- Agree the demurrage rate - part of the deal, in dollars per day of
-- delay. "Per day" is not a unit the system knows; it is the formula
-- in the invariant and the derived figure below. The zero-dollar seed
-- settlement opens the money book, same pattern as the cargo.
transformation agree_demurrage_rate(voyage, seed_settlement, daily):
    require VoyageFixture(voyage, _, _, _)
    require not DemurrageRate(voyage, _)
    admit DemurrageRate(voyage, daily)
    admit DemurrageSettled(seed_settlement, voyage, 0 USD)

-- Settle part (or all) of the demurrage. Only after the cargo work is
-- done - until then the counted time is still moving - and never past
-- what the delay is worth, which the invariant recomputes rather than
-- trusts. The amount is dollars by declaration: offer tonnes here and
-- the proposal does not type, let alone commit.
transformation settle_demurrage(settlement, voyage, amount):
    require CargoOpsCompleted(voyage, _)
    admit DemurrageSettled(settlement, voyage, amount)
    emit DemurrageSettlementRecorded(settlement)

-- The answer, computed rather than stored. Counted time is the sum of
-- the interval lengths; time on demurrage is whatever ran past the
-- allowance, floored at zero - `max` keeps a voyage that finished
-- early from showing negative demurrage. Because this is derived, it
-- can never disagree with the intervals it is built from: correct an
-- interval (next stage) and the answer moves with it, while anything
-- already settled on the old figure stands on its own record.
--
-- The domain requires the clock to have started: before commencement
-- there is nothing to count and no seed interval, so the question
-- "how much demurrage?" has no row yet rather than a wrong answer.
-- (Commencement admits the zero-length seed atomically, so once a
-- voyage appears here its duration sum is never empty.)
derived TimeOnDemurrage(voyage, allowed):
    over VoyageFixture(voyage, _, _, allowed) and LaytimeCommenced(voyage, _)
    value excess = max(sum(len | CountingInterval(_, voyage, _, _, len)) - allowed, duration(PT0S))

-- And what it is worth: the same excess, divided by twenty-four hours
-- into an exact count of days (5.5 days is 5.5, not "about five"),
-- times the agreed daily amount. Dollars out, because dollars times a
-- bare number is dollars - the only multiplication the units allow.
derived DemurrageDue(voyage, allowed, daily):
    over VoyageFixture(voyage, _, _, allowed) and DemurrageRate(voyage, daily) and LaytimeCommenced(voyage, _)
    value due = daily * (max(sum(len | CountingInterval(_, voyage, _, _, len)) - allowed, duration(PT0S)) / duration(PT24H))
